{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMxxLym3TFoRZAyKdT87by","type":"tool_result","content":"## Configuration\n\nConfiguration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can chan\n\n**Shared roles.** `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads role directories from another repository, so several repositories can reuse one role set. It is pinned like a sidecar: `ref` defaults to the shared repository's default branch (its remote HEAD, so `master` works too) and may be a branch, a tag or a full commit SHA. The controller fetches the ref shallowl\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by the committer\n```\n\n- A directory with `prompt.txt` is an agent role; a scanner directory with an executable `scan` is a deterministic scanner.\n- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n## Rollout\n\nStart on one repo (albertcbrown/ard itself, dogfooding; decided, #6) with the local executor and a human approval gate; humans merge until Gate 2, then the merger takes over. Widen only when the numbers say the loop is trustworthy. Gate 2 is run from [docs/runbooks/gate-2.md](runbooks/gate-2.md).\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>h\n    P1 -->|\"Gate 2: 10 merged PRs, failures under 10%\"| P2[\"Phase 2: All roles<br/>all triage roles<br/>all four reviewers<br/>review fix rounds<br/>merger loop<br/>remaining scanners\"]\n    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]\n    P3 --> P4[\"Phase 4: Docs and site<br/>user documentation<br/>GitHub Pages site\"]\n    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n```\n\nPhases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --\n\n**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock\n\nPhase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exer\n\n| Metric | Proposed target |\n| --- | --- |\n| Duplicate issues filed | 0 |\n| Executor failure rate | under 10% of jobs |\n| Scanner issues closed by an ard PR with no human code edits | 50% by the end of Phase 2 |\n| Median time from `ard:pending` to a ready PR | under 1 working day |\n| Cost per merged PR | measured in Phase 1 from the Total row of the issue's cost table; daily budget set from it |\n\n## Open questions\nbot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nversion.yml\n14:  protected_paths:\n15-    - .github/\n16-    - .ard/config.yaml\n17-    - .ard/scanners/\n18-    - .ard/triage/\n19-    - .ard/workers/\n20-    - .ard/reviewers/\n21-    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n22-    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab11da88-bedd-4a91-847e-cd60783376a2","uuid":"8506003b-5d56-445f-b9c0-8da265975282","timestamp":"2026-11-24T13:36:50.197Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Configuration\n\nConfiguration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can chan\n\n**Shared roles.** `roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads role directories from another repository, so several repositories can reuse one role set. It is pinned like a sidecar: `ref` defaults to the shared repository's default branch (its remote HEAD, so `master` works too) and may be a branch, a tag or a full commit SHA. The controller fetches the ref shallowl\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by the committer\n```\n\n- A directory with `prompt.txt` is an agent role; a scanner directory with an executable `scan` is a deterministic scanner.\n- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n## Rollout\n\nStart on one repo (albertcbrown/ard itself, dogfooding; decided, #6) with the local executor and a human approval gate; humans merge until Gate 2, then the merger takes over. Widen only when the numbers say the loop is trustworthy. Gate 2 is run from [docs/runbooks/gate-2.md](runbooks/gate-2.md).\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    P0[\"Phase 0: Skeleton<br/>controller and config<br/>label bootstrap<br/>committer, local executor<br/>cve and stale scanners<br/>dry-run mode\"] -->|\"Gate 1: 1 week dry-run, 0 duplicate issues\"| P1[\"Phase 1: First loop<br/>lead + engineer triage<br/>engineer worker<br/>eng-review<br/>Reaper<br/>h\n    P1 -->|\"Gate 2: 10 merged PRs, failures under 10%\"| P2[\"Phase 2: All roles<br/>all triage roles<br/>all four reviewers<br/>review fix rounds<br/>merger loop<br/>remaining scanners\"]\n    P2 -->|\"Gate 3: 50% merged with no human code edits\"| P3[\"Phase 3: Scale<br/>k8s executor<br/>second repo<br/>shared roles repo<br/>budgets and digest\"]\n    P3 --> P4[\"Phase 4: Docs and site<br/>user documentation<br/>GitHub Pages site\"]\n    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n```\n\nPhases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --\n\n**Where it runs (decided, #5; amended for the soak, [#144](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/144)).** `ard serve` runs live on the owner's Mac with the local executor until Phase 3, when it moves to the \"spark\" Kubernetes cluster with the k8s executor and a Lease lock in place of the `flock\n\nPhase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is exer\n\n| Metric | Proposed target |\n| --- | --- |\n| Duplicate issues filed | 0 |\n| Executor failure rate | under 10% of jobs |\n| Scanner issues closed by an ard PR with no human code edits | 50% by the end of Phase 2 |\n| Median time from `ard:pending` to a ready PR | under 1 working day |\n| Cost per merged PR | measured in Phase 1 from the Total row of the issue's cost table; daily budget set from it |\n\n## Open questions\nbot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nversion.yml\n14:  protected_paths:\n15-    - .github/\n16-    - .ard/config.yaml\n17-    - .ard/scanners/\n18-    - .ard/triage/\n19-    - .ard/workers/\n20-    - .ard/reviewers/\n21-    - \"**/CODEOWNERS\"\n22-    # Agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"returnCodeInterpretation":"No matches found","noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DMxxLym3TFoRZAyKdT87by","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
